title: Tutorial — How This Works
authors: Writing Journals tutorial, built in lo-blocks
description: Professor Ellington spends two minutes showing the student how the tool works — the conversation advances on Continue, activities land in the right pane, work saves as you type, and the tabs collect everything — then sends them to the Writers' Circle for their first journal.
~~~~

// HOW THE ACTIVITY PANE IS DRIVEN.
//
// Every card the conversation opens on the right is written as a TARGET EMBED:
//
//     ::tut_welcome [display=target:tut_sidebar label="Welcome"]
//
// which repoints the tut_sidebar UseHistory at that screen (once, the first
// time the line is revealed) AND leaves a small labelled button in the
// transcript at the point in the conversation where the card arrived. Clicking
// that button repoints the pane there again.
//
// In this tutorial that marker is not just plumbing — it is the lesson of the
// "Looking Back" section: the way back to an earlier activity is to find the
// moment in the conversation and click its button. The UseHistory arrows at the
// top of the pane still do the same job and get a one-line mention.
//
// Timing note: a set command executes while the reveal walks past it; an embed
// is content, so it is revealed as its own beat — the card lands one Continue
// BEFORE the line that talks about it, which is the order you want. Ellington
// glosses the button the first time one appears so it isn't unexplained noise.
//
// The Flash/FocusAction Triggers in tutorial_sba.olx watch @tut_sidebar.value.
// NOTE: an embed writes the QUALIFIED ref (edu.memphis.writing.sba/tut_welcome),
// not the bare name an arrow wrote — comparisons on this value must normalize
// both sides with DefinitionKey(...). Marker clicks re-write the value, so a
// revisit re-fires mode="each" cues.

How This Works
--------------
Ellington: before I hand you over to your first online journal, give me two minutes to show you around. [face=calm]

Ellington: over here on the left of the screen is the chat where you are part of a conversation. it moves when you click the **Continue** button below — which you've apparently already worked out. [face=smile]

::tut_welcome [display=target:tut_sidebar label="Welcome"]

Ellington: the right side is where the work happens. sometimes there will be text for you to read, sometimes we will ask you to interact with survey items, and sometimes you will do some writing over there. For now, read the text that just landed and then click **Mark as read** at the bottom when you are done. You will notice that the **Continue** button stays off (grey) until you do. The text is over there ---> [face=explaining]

Ellington: (that little **Welcome** button that just appeared here in the chat is a bookmark to it. ignore it for now — I'll come back to it.) [face=calm]

--- wait @tut_welcome_done.value ---

Try It Once
-----------
::tut_practice [display=target:tut_sidebar label="Try it"]

Ellington: good. when there's something to *do*, it shows up on the right side the same way, and it pulses to catch your eye. type anything at all in the box; one word is plenty. [face=explaining]

--- wait @tut_practice_text.value ---

Ellington: notice you never clicked "save." there is no save button anywhere here. every keystroke and mouse click is kept as you go. you could close this tab mid-sentence and your half-sentence would be waiting when you came back. [face=calm]

Looking Back
------------
// Taught by doing, like everything else here. BOTH ways back are taught as
// equals (Piotr): the bookmark button in the transcript, and the history bar
// at the top of the pane (‹ arrow, one dot per card seen, › arrow). The
// exercise accepts either route — a marker click and a ‹ click both write
// tut_sidebar.value (the marker writes the QUALIFIED ref, the bar re-writes
// whatever history holds), so the one DefinitionKey-normalized gate covers both.
Ellington: one more move, and it's the one people miss. there are two ways back to any page you've seen. remember that bookmark button? every page that lands on the right leaves one, right where it came up in our conversation. [face=explaining]

Ellington: also, see the small dots at the very top of the right side, between the two arrows? that's every page you've seen so far — the **‹** arrow walks back through them in order. [face=explaining]

Ellington: try either one — bring back the first page. scroll up and click **Welcome**, or click **‹** up top. [face=explaining]

// HACK: DefinitionKey(...) here is NOT long-term stable — do not copy to other
// courses. Same story as the cue triggers in tutorial_sba.olx; see bugs.md.
--- wait DefinitionKey(@tut_sidebar.value) === DefinitionKey('tut_welcome') ---

Ellington: the buttons, arrows, and dots let you revisit any earlier activity whenever you like, and looking back never undoes anything. click **Continue** and I'll bring you back to where we were. [face=smile]

The Tabs
--------
::tut_tabs_note [display=target:tut_sidebar label="The tabs"]

Ellington: last thing. the tabs along the top collect everything in one place and provide more information. in the writing journals, you can think of it like your 'running work'. it has information on the people you're talking to, important ideas that come up, and the page where you export a PDF to submit to your instructor at the end. [face=explaining]

Ellington: sometimes the conversation will point you to a tab that contains important information. but you can always come back to your work and you are free to click the tabs at any time. nothing 'locks' behind you. try the **Tips** tab up top if you're curious, then come back to **Main Content**. [face=smile]

That's Everything
-----------------
::tut_finish [display=target:tut_sidebar label="That's everything"]

Ellington: that's the whole machine. conversation on the left, work on the right, tabs up top. [face=smile]

Ellington: you did your first writing journal on paper. from here they move online. the next one is called "Who Am I As a Writer?", and three students are expecting you. soon, they will invite you to join them in what they call the Writers' Circle. tap **Continue** and I'll take you over. [face=calm]

--- pause ---

wj_course.selectedChild <- edu.memphis.writing.sba/wj1_sba_part1
